- Encrypting a drive adds a layer of security to protect your data in case someone gains unauthorized access to your computer or physically removes the drive. Below are methods for encrypting a drive on different operating systems using built-in tools.

1. Encrypting a Drive on Windows with BitLocker
BitLocker is available in Windows Pro and Enterprise editions. It offers full-disk encryption.

Steps to Encrypt Your Drive with BitLocker:
Open Control Panel:

Go to Start > Control Panel > System and Security > BitLocker Drive Encryption.
Turn on BitLocker:

Find the drive you want to encrypt (e.g., C: for system drive, D: for another partition) and click Turn on BitLocker.
Choose How to Unlock the Drive:

Choose a method to unlock the drive, such as:
Password
USB key (insert USB to unlock)
Backup Your Recovery Key:

BitLocker will generate a recovery key in case you forget your password.
You can save it to a Microsoft account, USB drive, file, or print it.
Choose Encryption Mode:

New encryption mode for SSDs or modern devices.
Compatible mode if you need to use the drive on older versions of Windows.
Start Encryption:

Click Start Encrypting. The process may take some time, depending on the size of your drive.
Reboot:

If encrypting the system drive, you may need to restart your computer.
BitLocker Recovery Key:
Ensure you securely store the recovery key, as it's essential to decrypt the drive in case you forget your password.